text.section:
The Board in making regulations; the Department in approving variances, control programs, or permits; and the courts in granting injunctive relief under the provisions of this chapter, shall consider facts and circumstances relevant to the reasonableness of the activity involved and the regulations proposed to control it, including:

The character and degree of injury to, or interference with, safety, health, or the reasonable use of property which is caused or threatened to be caused;


The social and economic value of the activity involved;


The suitability of the activity to the area in which it is located, except that consideration of this factor shall be satisfied if the local governing body of a locality in which a facility or activity is proposed has resolved that the location and operation of the proposed facility or activity is suitable to the area in which it is located; and


The scientific and economic practicality of reducing or eliminating the discharge resulting from such activity.
